vhost-user-gpu: Venus (Vulkan), blob resources and host-visible memory

Venus needs blob resources the guest maps (the host's Vulkan memory and the
shared memory of its command ring), contexts of the Venus capset and fences on
a context's timelines. QEMU's own virtio-gpu-gl has them, but runs virgl on
QEMU's main loop; vhost-user-gpu keeps the GPU off it and had none of them.

Frontend: a "hostmem" property like virtio-gpu's. The PCI device puts that
window in BAR 4 (virtio's shared memory capability, VIRTIO_GPU_SHM_ID_HOST_VISIBLE);
it becomes a virtio shared memory region (created after virtio_init(), which
sets up the list), so the backend can map files into it with vhost-user's
SHMEM_MAP. RESOURCE_BLOB and CONTEXT_INIT are offered when the backend has them.

vhost: files mapped with SHMEM_MAP stay out of the backend's memory table. The
backend has them already, and the table update after each mapping waited for
the backend while the backend waited for the reply to its next SHMEM_MAP
(deadlock on the second blob). SHMEM_MAP then commits the mapping before it
replies: the backend tells the guest the memory is there once it has the reply,
and the guest's first writes (Venus' ring) otherwise went nowhere.

Backend: --venus starts virglrenderer with Venus and its render server,
offers RESOURCE_BLOB, CONTEXT_INIT and vhost-user's SHMEM protocol feature,
lists the Venus capset, creates contexts with context_init, creates blobs
(with the guest's pages when they have some), maps a blob by exporting it
(a dma-buf for Vulkan memory, a memfd for shared memory) and asking the frontend
to map that fd, and answers fences on a context's rings
(write_context_fence). It starts virgl when the guest first reads the config:
the guest reads the number of capsets before it starts the device, and Venus'
capset only exists once the render server runs.

Tested with a Debian 13 guest (Mesa 25.0 Venus): vulkaninfo shows
"Virtio-GPU Venus (NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3080 Laptop GPU)", a GPU fill + copy into
host-visible memory reads back correctly, 20 runs in a row.

